course: dictionary
lesson: 8101
title: Rename: giving something a new name
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To rename something is to give it a new name. «The town renamed the park» means the park has a different name now, but it is still the same park in the same place.
explain: Computers use the same word. «Renaming a file» changes the name you see on the screen, but the words and pictures inside the file stay the same. Always ask what is being renamed: a name changes, but the thing itself does not.
explain: «Renaming a floor does not change its height.» In a hotel whose lift buttons go 12, 14, the floor marked 14 is really the 13th floor. Only the label changed.
explain: Maths uses rename in a different way. In US maths, «rename 6/8 as 3/4» means write the same amount in another form. The context tells you which meaning is used: a place, a file, or a number.
fact: Rename means to give someone or something a new name.
fact: «Rename a file» means change the name of a file or folder on a computer.
fact: In US maths, rename can mean write a number in an equal form, such as 6/8 as 3/4.
fact: «Be renamed as» or «be renamed after» tells you what the new name is. In the Bible story, Jacob was renamed Israel.
fact: Renaming a thing does not change the thing itself: a renamed file keeps its inside, and a renamed floor keeps its height.
exercise: The council decided that the old park would be renamed. What does renamed mean? | Pulled down and built again | Moved to a new town | Painted green | *Given a new name
exercise: «Renaming .mp3 to .png does not turn a song into a picture.» What changes when you rename a file? | The speed of the computer | *The name you see | The picture the file shows | The song inside the file
exercise: In US maths, «rename 6/8 as 3/4» means what? | *Write the same amount in another form | Count it in tens | Make the fraction bigger | Add the top and bottom numbers
test: «Businesses rename sad things to sound gentler: an undertaker becomes a funeral director.» Why do businesses rename things like this? | To make them harder to find | To make them sound louder | *To make them sound gentler | To make them sound sillier
test: «Renaming a floor does not change its height.» What has changed when the 13th floor is marked 14? | *Only its label, not its height | Its height, so it is higher up | The number of buttons in the lift | The name of the hotel
exam: Which sentence uses rename in the same way as «The town renamed the park»? | The class counted to ten | *The class renamed their pet hamster Biscuit | The class walked to the park | The class painted their classroom blue
